# Coding Standards for Rust

## Key Principles

- you MUST adhere to [Rust's naming conventions](https://rust-lang.github.io/api-guidelines/naming.html)
- you SHOULD embrace Rust's ownership and type system
- the standard library provides a great reference for best practices

## Tooling

- you SHOULD use or create `rust-toolchain.toml` files

|Check|Tool|Command|Notes|
|:-|:-|:-|:-|
|**Linting**|`clippy`|`cargo clippy --quiet --bins --tests --benches --no-deps -- -D warnings`|you MUST respect all lints defined in `Cargo.toml`; you MUST use `#[expect(...)]` instead of `#[allow(...)]`|
|**Formatting**|`rustfmt`|`cargo fmt --all --quiet --message-format=human --check`||
|**Documentation**|`rustdoc`|`RUSTDOCFLAGS="-D warnings" cargo doc --quiet --all-features --no-deps --document-private-items`|you SHOULD use intra-doc links; the flag turns broken links into errors|

## Paradigms

### Style

- you SHOULD prefer `core` over `::std`
- when no style is available yet or the style is incoherent, you MUST
  - prefix imports from external crates with `::` (i.e., `::tokio` instead of `tokio`)
  - import modules and not their item (i.e. `::std::process` instead of `::std::process::ExitCode`, then use `process::ExitCode`) unless an item is very frequently used
  - suffix trait imports that are not used by their name explicitly with `as _;` (i.e. `use ::std::error::Error as _;` instead of `use ::std::error::Error;`)

### Error Handling

- you MUST use `Result` and `Option` for error handling
- you SHOULD use the `?` operator to propagate errors, especially in async functions
- you MAY use `thiserror` or `anyhow` if they are already in the project; you MUST NOT add them by default

### Asynchronous Programming

- you SHOULD prefer `async fn` over `fn -> impl Future`
- you MUST use `.await` responsibly, ensuring safe points for context switching
- you MUST follow the project's existing async runtime; you MUST NOT add [`tokio`](https://docs.rs/crate/tokio/latest) (or another runtime) by default
- when the project already uses tokio, you MUST
  - use `::tokio::sync::Mutex` and `::tokio::sync::RwLock` for shared state across tasks instead of their standard-library counterparts
  - use `::tokio::task::yield_now` to yield control in cooperative multitasking scenarios
  - write unit tests with `tokio::test` for async tests
